    runtime: `Use RECEIVE → DECODE → INTERPRET → LEARN → ENCODE → TRANSMIT → OBSERVE when supported. SENSE, TRANSLATE, REPROGRAM, BROADCAST, and DISCOVER are product lanes, not separate engines. Customer data never trains a model or automatically changes production knowledge. Two-device recovery, sender identity, RF broadcast, and autonomous verification remain unverified.`,
    text: `ZionCore's founding product cycle has seven distinct stages. RECEIVE obtains a user-authorized sensor or file input and preserves its provenance. DECODE calculates measurable signal properties or recovers content only from a documented protocol that passes its validity checks. INTERPRET compares those results with relevant science, user-selected context, and optional spiritual perspectives. LEARN lets users compare saved records and supports only explicit, validated, owner-reviewed research updates; customer data never trains a model or changes production knowledge automatically. ENCODE transforms user-selected text or a recording into a documented signal format. TRANSMIT means a user-authorized output through a supported device or downloadable signal. OBSERVE records follow-up measurements and compares them with baselines or controls, reporting uncertainty rather than treating a match as proof of an intentional reply.

The product names SENSE, TRANSLATE, REPROGRAM, BROADCAST, and DISCOVER describe connected product lanes, not five autonomous engines. SENSE maps to authorized capture and import. TRANSLATE maps to local measurement, supported protocol decoding, and optional AI or user-chosen spiritual interpretation. REPROGRAM maps to user-directed message encoding and waveform preparation. BROADCAST maps to user-controlled acoustic playback or export. DISCOVER maps to comparison and research proposals. Keep each lane bounded by the actual devices, protocols, permissions, and service connections implemented.

For the current product, microphone and supported local-file inputs yield acoustic samples; local processing can report frequency bins, peaks, harmonics, timing, and signal features. The interpretation request uses validated derived features and user-selected context. The browser can create and decode its supported local text/prayer packet format. Saved history can support comparison when the user selects records and the configured storage path is available. The Device Centre can import supported digital recordings and sensor tables, but imported samples do not verify a physical receiver or its calibration. Direct RF, ultrasound, vibration, and plant-sensor reception require compatible hardware and validated measurements. Real two-device message recovery, sender identity, encryption, continuous broadcasting, autonomous acquisition of external information, and model retraining are not implied by the seven-stage cycle.

Every answer should implicitly or explicitly locate itself in the cycle. “Measured” belongs to RECEIVE or DECODE; “candidate cause” belongs to INTERPRET; “user-selected comparison” belongs to LEARN; encoded bytes belong to ENCODE; a user-started audio output belongs to TRANSMIT; a follow-up measurement belongs to OBSERVE. A packet checksum verifies frame integrity, not sender identity or delivery. A user's reflection belongs to personal meaning. If a stage is missing, say which one is missing and what evidence, device, or consent would be needed to advance. Never make the product appear less capable than its real features, but never let an architecture diagram create the impression that a future stage is already connected.

The stages inform incremental development. A useful next capability is one that can be specified, permissioned, built, tested with known inputs, and maintained. Do not jump from a plausible research idea to an operational claim. A device integration needs a published measurement range, sampling path, calibration process, failure status, and end-to-end test. A memory feature needs user control, data ownership rules, retention/deletion behavior, and an explanation of how a saved record affects future results. A transmitter needs output-level limits, a documented protocol, consent for playback, and a means to stop it immediately. A verification feature needs a defined outcome and a control condition.`,
